  paired sample n=2694 · waves [1991, 1998, 2008, 2018] — **four waves, and four is four**

=== (1) THE OBSERVED MOTION — per wave, and the marginals beside it ===
  1991  n= 723  res_homo 0.845  res_abort 0.620  **Δ +0.225**   | marginals: mean(homo wrong) 2.34 · mean(ab wrong) 2.36 · share(law allow) 0.49
  1998  n= 679  res_homo 0.755  res_abort 0.450  **Δ +0.305**   | marginals: mean(homo wrong) 1.84 · mean(ab wrong) 2.30 · share(law allow) 0.43
  2008  n= 606  res_homo 0.800  res_abort 0.269  **Δ +0.532**   | marginals: mean(homo wrong) 1.61 · mean(ab wrong) 2.44 · share(law allow) 0.48
  2018  n= 686  res_homo 0.788  res_abort 0.401  **Δ +0.387**   | marginals: mean(homo wrong) 1.13 · mean(ab wrong) 2.43 · share(law allow) 0.50
  **observed spread of Δ = 0.307**  (max−min over four waves)
  ⚠ a max−min of four order statistics is biased upward by noise ⇒ its null must be the SPREAD OF THE SAME STATISTIC under the null world, never zero.

=== (2) THE NULL — kind of null NAMED: an IPF-reconstructed CONSTANT-COUPLING null ===
  each wave rebuilt from the POOLED table onto ITS OWN margins ⇒ every odds ratio preserved,
  every marginal drift reproduced. **The null world is `one coupling, four marginals`** —
  i.e. worlds A and D TOGETHER WITH the marginal-drift rival M.
  null spread of res_homo : median 0.106  95th **0.153**
  null spread of res_abort: median 0.092  95th **0.159**
  null spread of Δ (upper bound = sum of the two): median 0.201  95th **0.285**
  observed: res_homo spread **0.089** (null 95th 0.153) · res_abort spread **0.351** (null 95th 0.159) · Δ spread **0.307** (null 95th 0.285)
  => Δ spread above its null: **True** · abortion's own spread above its null: **True**

=== (3) POSITIVE CONTROL — a synthetic world with a genuinely MOVING coupling ===
  dose g rotates the association across waves; at g=0 the coupling is CONSTANT while the real
  marginal drift is still applied — so a control that fires at g=0 would be measuring drift.
  g=0.00  simulated spread = 0.117
  g=0.25  simulated spread = 0.323
  g=0.50  simulated spread = 0.666
  g=0.75  simulated spread = 0.774
  g=1.00  simulated spread = 0.717
  measured TURNING POINT at g=0.75 (spread 0.774) — beyond it the plant inverts the association and `1−ρ²` comes back down, because the statistic is EVEN IN ρ. Monotonicity is required UP TO the turning point, not past it.
  => positive control **PASS** — at g=0 the spread is 0.117, INSIDE the null (0.159); at the turning point it is 0.774, outside; monotone up to it: True

=== (4) NEGATIVE CONTROL — does the machinery manufacture motion from nothing? ===
  the IPF reconstruction re-measured: median spread 0.092 vs its own 95th 0.159 -> **PASS** (the null does not sit on its own tail)

=== (5) SPECIFICATION CURVE — estimators that respond DIFFERENTLY to marginal change ===
  cells: **12** (3 estimators × 4 sanction measures)
  gamma     Δ spread median 0.376  range [0.307,0.404]  Δ>0 in every wave: False
  kendall   Δ spread median 0.292  range [0.275,0.293]  Δ>0 in every wave: True
  spearman  Δ spread median 0.307  range [0.289,0.308]  Δ>0 in every wave: True
  ⚠ γ and τ-b respond differently to marginal change than ρ; if the motion were drift it should SHRINK under γ. The whole grid is printed, including cells that disagree.

